<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-graphs" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Graph Theory</title>

  <introduction>
      <p>
    You meet your friend again and you hear more interesting things:
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          In any group of people, there are two who have the same number of
          friends.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Six degrees of separation: for any two people in the world, one can
          find a chain of at most six connections/mutual acquaintances.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Chemistry: An acyclic saturated hydrocarbon or alkane or paraffin
          <m>C_{m}H_{n}</m> (a substance made only of carbon and hydrogen)
          exists if and only if <m>m=2n+2</m>.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Friendship paradox: On average, your friends have more friends than
          you do.
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Any map can be colored with four colors such that any two countries
          sharing a border, receive different colors.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
      </p>
  </introduction>

